## Proof

We work with the Fourier transform convention
$$\hat{f}(\xi)=\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}f(x)\,e^{-i\xi x}\,dx,\qquad f(x)=\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}\hat{f}(\xi)\,e^{i\xi x}\,d\xi.$$

The given distribution is
$$f(x)=\frac{2\pi\,\delta(x)}{e^{ix}+1}.$$

**Step 1: Simplify the distribution.**

The Dirac delta $\delta(x)$ is supported at the single point $x=0$. The function $g(x)=e^{ix}+1$ is smooth (entire, in fact) and satisfies
$$g(0)=e^{i\cdot 0}+1=1+1=2\neq 0.$$

For any smooth function $g$ with $g(0)\neq 0$, the product of a distribution $\delta(x)$ with $\frac{1}{g(x)}$ is well-defined by the standard multiplication rule for distributions by smooth functions:
$$\frac{\delta(x)}{g(x)}=\frac{1}{g(0)}\,\delta(x).$$

Applying this with $g(x)=e^{ix}+1$:
$$\frac{\delta(x)}{e^{ix}+1}=\frac{1}{e^{i\cdot 0}+1}\,\delta(x)=\frac{1}{2}\,\delta(x).$$

Therefore
$$f(x)=2\pi\cdot\frac{1}{2}\,\delta(x)=\pi\,\delta(x).$$

**Step 2: Compute the Fourier transform.**

Using the sifting property of the delta distribution,
$$\hat{f}(\xi)=\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}\pi\,\delta(x)\,e^{-i\xi x}\,dx=\pi\,e^{-i\xi\cdot 0}=\pi.$$

**Verification.** The inverse Fourier transform of the constant $\pi$ is
$$\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}\pi\,e^{i\xi x}\,d\xi=\frac{1}{2}\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}e^{i\xi x}\,d\xi=\frac{1}{2}\cdot 2\pi\,\delta(x)=\pi\,\delta(x)=f(x),$$
confirming consistency.

**Conclusion.** The Fourier transform of $\dfrac{2\pi\,\delta(x)}{e^{ix}+1}$ is the constant function

$$\boxed{\hat{f}(\xi)=\pi.}$$

